# WebSockets

An ordinary request gets one response and is over. A WebSocket stays open,
and either side can send a message at any time. That makes it the tool for
anything the server needs to push: chat, notifications, live prices, a
progress bar.

If only the server needs to push, and the browser just listens,
[server-sent events](/docs/controllers/server-sent-events) are simpler:
they are plain HTTP, and the browser reconnects by itself.

In Howdy a WebSocket is an ordinary route. It starts life as a `GET`
request, so it is declared on a controller like any other and passes
through the same middleware, [guards](/docs/controllers/guards) and
versioning. The handler describes the socket, and finishes with
`websocket.upgrade` where another handler would return a page.

## An echo server

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type Context}
import howdy/websocket

pub fn echo_controller() {
  controller.new("echo")
  |> controller.get("/", fn(ctx: Context) {
    websocket.new(fn(_socket) { 0 })
    |> websocket.on_text(fn(socket, count, text) {
      let _ = websocket.send_text(socket, text)
      websocket.continue(count + 1)
    })
    |> websocket.upgrade(ctx)
  })
}
```

In a browser, `new WebSocket("ws://localhost:8787/echo")` connects to it,
and every message sent comes straight back.

There are three parts to every socket:

- `websocket.new` takes the function to run when the connection opens. What
  it returns is the socket's starting state, here a count of `0`.
- Callbacks such as `on_text` handle what happens next. Each is given the
  socket to send on and the current state.
- Each callback returns what to do now: `websocket.continue(state)` to carry
  on with a new state, or `websocket.close` to end the connection.

State is how a socket remembers things between messages. It can be any type
you like: a count, a user, a record. Each connection runs in its own
process, so its state is private to it, and a crash in one connection does
not touch any other.

## Receiving messages

- `on_text` handles text frames, as a `String`.
- `on_binary` handles binary frames, as a `BitArray`.
- `on_json` handles text frames by decoding them as JSON first, and gives
  the callback the decoded value.

Frames with no callback are ignored. Most apps speak JSON, so `on_json` is
the usual choice:

```gleam
import gleam/dynamic/decode

pub type ChatMessage {
  ChatMessage(body: String)
}

fn chat_message_decoder() {
  use body <- decode.field("body", decode.string)
  decode.success(ChatMessage(body:))
}
```

```gleam
|> websocket.on_json(chat_message_decoder(), fn(socket, state, message) {
  // message is a ChatMessage here.
  websocket.continue(state)
})
```

`on_json` ignores frames that fail to decode. `on_json_or` takes a second
callback for those, if you would rather close the connection. `on_json`
replaces any `on_text` callback, since both handle text frames.

## Sending messages

Send on the socket with `send_text`, `send_binary` or `send_json`:

```gleam
let _ = websocket.send_json(socket, json.object([#("price", json.int(1250))]))
```

Each returns a `Result`, which is an error when the client has gone away.
There is rarely anything useful to do about that, since the connection is
about to close and `on_close` will run, so it is usually ignored with
`let _ =`.

## Closing

`websocket.close(socket, code, reason)` sends the client a close frame and
ends the connection. The code is one of the standard close codes in
`websocket.CloseCode`:

```gleam
websocket.close(socket, websocket.NormalClosure, "goodbye")
```

`NormalClosure` is the usual choice. `InvalidPayloadData` suits a message
you could not understand, and `PolicyViolation` one that broke your rules.
`ApplicationCode(code)` carries a code of your own, between 3000 and 4999.
`websocket.stop()` ends the connection without telling the client why.

`on_close` runs once when a connection ends, however that happens: your
callback closed it, the client closed it, or the network dropped. Use it to
tidy up. Nothing can be sent from there, because the connection is gone.

## Channels

A socket callback can only send to its own client. To reach other clients,
use `howdy/websocket/channel`. A socket joins a topic, and anything in your
program can broadcast to every socket on that topic.

This is a complete chat room. The room comes from the path and the name
from the query string, read with the same helpers as any other handler:

```gleam
import gleam/json
import howdy/param
import howdy/query
import howdy/websocket/channel

fn chat(ctx: Context) {
  use room <- param.string(ctx, "room")
  use name <- query.string_or(ctx, "name", default: "anonymous")
  let topic = "room:" <> room

  websocket.new(fn(socket) {
    channel.join(socket, topic)
    channel.broadcast_text(topic, name <> " joined")
    name
  })
  |> websocket.on_json_or(
    chat_message_decoder(),
    fn(_socket, name, message: ChatMessage) {
      channel.broadcast_json(
        topic,
        json.object([
          #("from", json.string(name)),
          #("body", json.string(message.body)),
        ]),
      )
      websocket.continue(name)
    },
    fn(socket, _name, _error) {
      websocket.close(socket, websocket.InvalidPayloadData, "expected json")
    },
  )
  |> websocket.on_close(fn(_socket, name) {
    channel.broadcast_text(topic, name <> " left")
  })
  |> websocket.upgrade(ctx)
}
```

A broadcast reaches every socket on the topic, including the one that sent
it, so the sender sees their own message arrive like everyone else's.

Things worth knowing about channels:

- A topic is just a string. Nothing needs creating first; a topic exists
  while it has members.
- A socket that closes or crashes leaves all its topics automatically, so
  the chat room above needs no `channel.leave`. Call `leave` when a socket
  should stop listening but stay open.
- `broadcast` returns at once. Each socket sends from its own process, so
  one slow client never holds up the others, or the caller.
- `channel.size(topic)` tells you how many sockets are on a topic.
- If your app runs on several connected Erlang nodes, a broadcast reaches
  the sockets on all of them.

Broadcasting is not limited to sockets. An ordinary HTTP handler, a service
or a background job can do it too:

```gleam
fn announce(ctx: Context) {
  channel.broadcast_text("room:lobby", "Server restarting in five minutes")
  controller.status(ctx, 202)
}
```

## Presence

A channel knows which sockets are on a topic, but not who they belong to.
`howdy/websocket/presence` tracks people: each socket says who it is with a
key, such as a user id, and a meta value describing them, such as a name or
whether they are typing. Someone with the page open in two tabs is one key
with two metas, present until the last tab closes.

Describe a kind of presence once, with how its meta goes to and from JSON:

```gleam
import howdy/websocket/presence.{type Presence}

pub type Here {
  Here(name: String, typing: Bool)
}

pub fn people() -> Presence(Here) {
  presence.new(
    "people",
    encode: fn(here: Here) {
      json.object([
        #("name", json.string(here.name)),
        #("typing", json.bool(here.typing)),
      ])
    },
    decoder: {
      use name <- decode.field("name", decode.string)
      use typing <- decode.field("typing", decode.bool)
      decode.success(Here(name:, typing:))
    },
  )
}
```

Then track the socket and watch the room when it opens. Callbacks run in the
socket's own process, so `process.self()` is the socket:

```gleam
websocket.new(fn(socket) {
  presence.track(people(), process.self(), topic, key: name, meta: Here(name:, typing: False))
  presence.watch(people(), socket, topic)
  name
})
```

Tracking the same socket, topic and key again replaces its meta, which is
how a status such as typing changes. A socket that closes or crashes leaves
by itself, like it leaves its channels.

A watching socket is sent everyone on the topic straight away, then a diff
of joins and leaves after every change. The browser keeps the list with a
small script that Howdy ships. Mount it as a controller:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(presence.client(at: "/howdy/presence.js"))
```

and hand it every message from the socket:

```js
import { Presence } from "/howdy/presence.js"

const people = new Presence({ name: "people", topic: "room:lobby" })
  .onSync((list) => render(list))   // [{ key, metas }]
  .onJoin((key, current, joined) => { if (!current) console.log(key, "arrived") })
  .onLeave((key, remaining, left) => { if (remaining.length === 0) console.log(key, "left") })

socket.onmessage = (event) => {
  const message = JSON.parse(event.data)
  if (people.receive(message)) return
  // your own messages
}
```

Server code reads the same information:

- `presence.list(people(), topic)` returns every key on the topic with its
  metas, and `presence.count` the number of keys.
- `presence.subscribe(people(), topic, subject, map)` sends a message made
  from each diff to a subject, for a live view or a service that reacts to
  people arriving and leaving. It returns the current list, so nothing is
  missed between reading it and the first diff.

Presence works across a cluster. Each node keeps its own sockets' presences
and shares them with the other nodes, so `list` answers for every node from
memory. If a node goes away its people leave everywhere else, and if it
comes back they rejoin.

## Messages from your own code

Channels send frames to clients. Sometimes you want to send a value to the
socket's own code instead, and let it decide what to do. `websocket.subject`
gives you a `Subject` for the connection, and whatever is sent to it arrives
at the `on_message` callback:

```gleam
pub type Push {
  PriceChanged(Int)
  Shutdown
}

fn prices(ctx: Context) {
  websocket.new(fn(socket) {
    price_feed.register(websocket.subject(socket))
    Nil
  })
  |> websocket.on_message(fn(socket, state, message) {
    case message {
      PriceChanged(pence) -> {
        let _ =
          websocket.send_json(socket, json.object([#("price", json.int(pence))]))
        websocket.continue(state)
      }
      Shutdown -> websocket.close(socket, websocket.GoingAway, "server restarting")
    }
  })
  |> websocket.upgrade(ctx)
}
```

Here `price_feed.register` stands for your own code, which keeps the subject
and later calls `process.send(subject, PriceChanged(1250))`. The message
type is yours, so the socket can react differently to each variant, and
update its state, which a broadcast frame cannot do.

## Who may connect

A WebSocket is open to a kind of attack an ordinary request is not. Any
website can open a socket to your server from a visitor's browser, and the
browser sends your cookies along with it. So before upgrading, Howdy checks
the `origin` header that browsers attach:

- By default only your own site may connect: the origin must have the same
  scheme, host and port as the request. Anything else answers `403`.
- Behind a proxy that terminates TLS, such as Traefik, Caddy or nginx, your
  app sees plain HTTP. The check then takes the scheme from the proxy's
  `x-forwarded-proto` header, and the host from `x-forwarded-host` if it
  sends one, so it works with no configuration. A hostile page cannot set
  those headers on a visitor's handshake, so this gives nothing away.
- A request with no `origin` header is allowed, because command line tools
  and other servers do not send one. Add `websocket.require_origin` to
  refuse those too, for an endpoint only browsers should use.
- If your pages are served from somewhere else, such as a separate
  frontend, list their origins exactly:

```gleam
websocket.new(on_open)
|> websocket.allow_origins(["https://app.example.com"])
|> websocket.require_origin
|> websocket.upgrade(ctx)
```

The origin check stops other websites using your visitors' sessions. It
does not tell you who the visitor is, so it goes alongside authentication,
not in place of it. Protect a socket route with a
[guard](/docs/controllers/guards) like any other. One difference: browsers
cannot set headers on a WebSocket handshake, so a socket guard reads a
cookie or a query parameter, not an `authorization` header.

## Testing sockets

`howdy/testing` has no connection to upgrade, so a socket route that gets as
far as `upgrade` answers `426 Upgrade Required` there. That is enough to
test everything in front of the socket, which is where most mistakes are:
the guard, the parameters and the origin check.

```gleam
import howdy/testing

pub fn chat_is_reachable_test() {
  let res = testing.get("/chat/lobby") |> testing.send(app())
  assert res.status == 426
}

pub fn other_sites_are_refused_test() {
  let res =
    testing.get("/chat/lobby")
    |> testing.header("origin", "https://evil.example")
    |> testing.send(app())
  assert res.status == 403
}
```

To keep the conversation itself testable, put the logic in plain functions
that take a state and a message and return the new state, and call those
from the callbacks.
